WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 172: Herbert Hoover: The Great Humanitarian, Belgian Relief, and the Mississippi Flood (1914–1928)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.000
[Rebecca] Today, we rediscover the extraordinary, forgotten early life of Herbert Hoover.

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:28.080
[Rebecca] Decades before he was vilified during the economic darkness of the 1930s, Hoover was hailed as the Napoleon of Mercy, a self-made mining engineer who single-handedly rescued entire nations from starvation during and after the First World War.

5
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:38.240
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the orphan of West Branch, the Belgian food relief miracle, and the master of emergencies.

6
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:46.560
[Rebecca] Herbert Clark Hoover was born in August 1874 in a tiny, two-room Borden Batten cottage in West Branch, Iowa.

7
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:54.160
[George] His parents were devout Quakers who practiced strict pacifism, communal charity, and the virtues of silent contemplation.

8
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.120
[George] But tragedy struck with terrifying speed.

9
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.120
[George] His father Jesse, a village blacksmith, died of rheumatic fever when Herbert was only six.

10
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.280
[George] Three years later, his mother Hulda, an eloquent Quaker minister, succumbed to typhoid fever.

11
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:16.640
[George] Orphaned at age nine and separated from his brother and sister, Herbert was sent to Oregon to live with an austere uncle.

12
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:26.400
[George] The silent, traumatized boy developed an iron will, a ferocious work ethic, and an absolute determination to conquer adversity through practical science.

13
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:38.160
[George] In 1891, hearing of a brand new, tuition free university opening in California called Stanford, 17-year-old Hoover enrolled in its very first pioneer class.

14
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:52.720
[George] Majoring in geology and mining engineering, he worked in the university laundry, ran a student newspaper, and met the love of his life, Lou Henry, a brilliant fellow geology student who would become his lifelong intellectual partner.

15
00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:01.080
[George] Graduating in 1895 with $40 dollars in his pocket, Hoover started at the bottom as a common mucker in a Nevada county gold mine.

16
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:14.920
[George] Those grueling underground shifts taught Hoover the real value of human labor, cementing his conviction that engineering, precision, and relentless personal discipline could overcome any obstacle in the physical or human world.

17
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:24.280
[George] That pioneer engineering experience instilled in Hoover a lifelong faith in quantitative analysis, human ingenuity, and practical organization.

18
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:30.120
[George] Hoover's meteoric ascent in the international mining industry was nothing short of miraculous.

19
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:45.880
[George] Hired at age 23 by a distinguished British mining syndicate, he was dispatched to the sun-baked, waterless goldfields of Western Australia, where he identified the historic sons of Gualia Mine, which yielded tens of millions of dollars in bullion.

20
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:53.880
[George] In 1899, newly married to Lou Henry, Hoover accepted the post of chief engineer for China's Bureau of Mines.

21
00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:02.360
[George] While stationed in the northern port city of Tianjin in the summer of 1900, the anti-Western Boxer Rebellion erupted with savage fury.

22
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:10.840
[George] For a harrowing month, Tianjin was besieged by thousands of armed Chinese insurgents who bombarded the foreign compound with artillery.

23
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:26.360
[George] While Liu volunteered as a nurse under fire, 26-year-old Herbert Hoover organized food supply lines, directed water distribution, and constructed fortified barricades of sugar and rice bags to shield women and children from shell fragments.

24
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:37.560
[George] In the decade that followed, Hoover established himself as the premier international mining consultant in the world, maintaining luxurious offices in London, San Francisco, and St.

25
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.680
[George] Petersburg.

26
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:52.800
[George] By age 40, he was worth a fortune of $4 million dollars, equivalent to more than 100 million today, yet he was seized by a restless, burning desire to dedicate his organizational genius to the service of humanity.

27
00:03:52.800 --> 00:04:01.520
[George] From the gold mines of Australia to the mountains of China, Hoover proved that technical skill combined with courage could overcome any crisis.

28
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:09.920
[George] In August 1914, the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand hurled Europe into the cataclysm of the First World War.

29
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:22.800
[George] When the Imperial German army swept through neutral Belgium, the civilian population of seven million Belgians and two million French citizens in the occupied Northern Territory was trapped behind military lines.

30
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:35.920
[George] Belgium imported 80% of its food, cut off by the British naval blockade and subjected to German army requisitions, the entire nation faced imminent, catastrophic starvation within six weeks.

31
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.720
[George] World leaders threw up their hands in despair, caught in diplomatic deadlock.

32
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.640
[George] Hoover did not hesitate.

33
00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:53.200
[George] Relinquishing his entire global business empire and refusing to accept a single cent in compensation, Hoover founded the Commission for Relief in Belgium, the CRB.

34
00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:06.640
[George] Operating as a de facto independent sovereign state with its own fleet of 35 cargo ships flying a neutral CRB flag, Hoover raised hundreds of millions of dollars from private philanthropy and foreign governments.

35
00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:18.800
[George] He personally negotiated with both the Kaiser's top generals in Berlin and British naval commanders in London, guaranteeing that food delivered to Belgian civilians would never be seized by German troops.

36
00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:27.680
[George] For four continuous years, Hoover delivered five million metric tons of wheat, milk, and medical supplies across mined oceans.

37
00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:35.960
[George] It was the largest non-governmental humanitarian operation in world history, run with zero overhead and perfect logistical precision.

38
00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:48.440
[George] Establishing an enduring standard for international crisis relief, the Commission for Relief in Belgium became the gold standard of international humanitarian action, saving a generation from extinction.

39
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:59.000
[George] When the United States entered the war in April 1917, President Woodrow Wilson immediately summoned Hoover to Washington to serve as the United States food administrator.

40
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:09.240
[George] Hoover rejected mandatory rationing, price controls, and bureaucratic policing, relying instead on moral suasion and voluntary civic patriotism.

41
00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:20.840
[George] Coining the rallying cry Food will win the war, he mobilized 20 million American housewives behind meatless Mondays, wheatless Wednesdays, and backyard victory gardens.

42
00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:33.800
[George] Americans slashed domestic food waste, doubled agricultural exports to Allied soldiers in Europe, and created a popular new verb, to Hooverize meant to conserve and economize for the common good.

43
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:43.080
[George] When the armistice was signed in November 1918, a new and far deadlier threat arose: the total economic collapse of Central and Eastern Europe.

44
00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:55.000
[George] As director general of the American Relief Administration, Hoover organized emergency food trains that kept 40 million people from Poland to Armenia from perishing in the winter of 1919.

45
00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:06.200
[George] In 1921, when devastating drought brought terrifying famine to Bolshevik Russia, Hoover extended relief to the Soviet people despite his vehement hatred of communism.

46
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:13.880
[George] When critics demanded to know why he was feeding the citizens of an enemy regime, Hoover gave an answer that should echo through the ages.

47
00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:16.320
[George] 20 million people are starving.

48
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:18.960
[George] Whatever their politics, they shall be fed.

49
00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:28.000
[George] Maxim Gorky, the celebrated Russian author, wrote to Hoover that his generosity had saved an entire generation of Russian children from the grave.

50
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:37.040
[George] His selfless relief efforts across wartorn Europe and famine-stricken Russia earned him universal acclaim as the premier savior of human lives.

51
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.000
[George] In 1921, President Warren G.

52
00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:49.920
[George] Harding appointed Hoover Secretary of Commerce, a sleepy cabinet backwater that Hoover instantly transformed into the most dynamic engine of modern federal governance.

53
00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:57.200
[George] Reappointed by Calvin Coolidge, Hoover was dubbed the Secretary of Commerce and Under Secretary of Everything Else.

54
00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:14.480
[George] He believed passionately in associationalism, the idea that government should act as a helpful partner to private industry, encouraging voluntary cooperation, eliminating industrial waste, and fostering economic efficiency without resorting to authoritarian regulation.

55
00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:27.760
[George] Hoover standardized thousands of manufactured products, from uniform brick sizes and milk bottle caps to standard plumbing threads and lumber dimensions, saving the American economy billions of dollars annually.

56
00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:44.160
[George] Recognizing the transformative future of technology, he established the federal regulatory framework for commercial civil aviation, drafted the Radio Act of 1927 to prevent chaotic broadcast interference, and laid the engineering groundwork for the sent.

57
00:08:44.160 --> 00:09:03.720
[George] Lawrence Seaway and the colossal Boulder Dam project on the Colorado River, a monumental engineering Marvel Hoover envisioned a modern technocratic republic where science, efficiency, and private public cooperation solved poverty and waste without ever requiring coercive state decrees or military intervention.

58
00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:12.760
[George] As Secretary of Commerce, Hoover laid the technological and regulatory foundations that enabled the modern American economic miracle to take flight.

59
00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:25.000
[George] In the spring of 1927, the swollen waters of the Mississippi River broke through levee after levy, unleashing the most catastrophic natural disaster in United States history up to that time.

60
00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:41.160
[George] The flood submerged 27,000 square miles across seven southern states, an area equal to Massachusetts, Connecticut, and Vermont combined, submerging towns under 30 feet of murky water, leaving 630,000 people homeless.

61
00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.720
[George] And paralyzing the regional economy.

62
00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:50.760
[George] When local authorities were overwhelmed, President Coolidge appointed Herbert Hoover to head a special cabinet relief commission.

63
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:57.000
[George] Hoover arrived in Memphis and took absolute personal command of the disaster zone with military precision.

64
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:10.680
[George] Mobilizing a fleet of 800 motorboats, 200 aircraft, the American Red Cross, and the Army Corps of Engineers, Hoover established 150 massive, sanitary tent cities on high ground.

65
00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:23.800
[George] He fed and sheltered over 600,000 destitute citizens for four months, successfully vaccinated more than 300,000 against typhoid, and prevented the outbreak of deadly post flood epidemics.

66
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:34.600
[George] Millions of grateful Americans hailed Hoover as the undisputed master of emergencies, a modern magician who could solve any crisis through the power of science, organization.

67
00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] And his extraordinary performance during the flood convinced Americans of all walks of life that Herbert Hoover was the single most capable and compassionate public servant of the 20th century.

68
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:55.520
[George] His masterly response to the 1927 flood disaster cemented his reputation as an administrative genius who could overcome any natural catastrophe.

69
00:10:55.520 --> 00:11:05.040
[George] When Calvin Coolidge announced he would not seek another term, Herbert Hoover was the irresistible, overwhelming choice of the American people for the presidency.

70
00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:18.160
[George] Nominated on the first ballot at the 1928 Republican National Convention in Kansas City, Hoover stood before the electorate not as a calculating politician, but as a brilliant, nonpartisan engineer statesman.

71
00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:21.680
[George] His Democratic opponent was New York Governor Alfred E.

72
00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:29.360
[George] Smith, a charismatic urban Roman Catholic whose anti-prohibition stance alienated rural Protestant voters.

73
00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:34.320
[George] In the November election, Hoover achieved a historic, crushing landslide.

74
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:46.880
[George] He captured 40 states, won 58% of the popular vote, and shattered the previously impregnable, solid South by carrying five Southern states that had not voted Republicans since Reconstruction.

75
00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:54.640
[George] On March 4, 1929, Herbert Hoover took the oath of office in the freezing rain on the east portico of the Capitol.

76
00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:02.960
[George] As he declared in his inaugural address, the United States stood nearer to the final triumph over poverty than ever before in human history.

77
00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:12.320
[George] Yet seven months later, the golden horizon vanished into the abyss of the Great Depression, unleashing a tragedy that would utterly engulf his presidency.

78
00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:21.520
[George] Entering the presidency on a wave of unprecedented popular adoration, Hoover seemed destined to lead the nation into a golden age of prosperity.

79
00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:33.000
[Rebecca] Herbert Hoover entered the White House as the most admired living American, a global savior whose engineering genius had fed millions and conquered historic disasters.

80
00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:40.600
[Rebecca] But within seven short months of his inauguration, the foundations of the global financial system cracked wide open.

81
00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:57.000
[Rebecca] Next week, we examine the darkest chapter of Hoover's life Black Tuesday, the Wall Street Crash of 1929, the emergence of Hoovervilles, the tragic clash with the bonus army veterans at Anacostra Flats, and the tragic fall of a great humanitarian.

82
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:02.120
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

